   "d": 4,
   "title": {
    "ar": "any وunknown وas const وsatisfies",
    "en": "any, unknown, as const and satisfies"
   },
   "goal": {
    "ar": "تتعامل مع البيانات الغريبة من غير ما تطفي الحماية.",
    "en": "Handle unknown data without switching off the protection."
   },
   "learn": [
    {
     "h": {
      "ar": "any مقابل unknown",
      "en": "any vs unknown"
     },
     "p": {
      "ar": "**any** = «سيبني في حالي»: tsc بيبطّل يفحص — كل حاجة مسموحة، والأخطاء بترجع. **unknown** = «مش عارف لسه»: لازم تفحص قبل ما تستخدم. JSON جاي من API أو webhook = unknown، وبعد التحقق يبقى نوعك. وفعّل `noImplicitAny` (جوه strict).",
      "en": "**any** = «leave me alone»: tsc stops checking — everything is allowed and bugs come back. **unknown** = «not known yet»: you must check before use. JSON from an API or webhook = unknown, and after validation it becomes your type. And turn on `noImplicitAny` (part of strict)."
     },
     "ex": "type Order = { id: number; total: number };\nfunction isOrder(x: unknown): x is Order {\n  return typeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && typeof (x as Record<string, unknown>).id === \"number\" && typeof (x as Record<string, unknown>).total === \"number\";\n}\nconst body: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"id\": 101, \"total\": 250}');\n// body.total   ← tsc: 'body' is of type 'unknown'\nif (isOrder(body)) console.log(\"valid order\", body.id, body.total);\n\nconst risky: any = JSON.parse('{\"id\": 102}');\nconsole.log(\"any lets this through:\", typeof risky.total);   // \"undefined\" — risky.total.toFixed(2) would crash",
     "node": 1,
     "ts": 1
    },
    {
     "h": {
      "ar": "as والـ assertions",
      "en": "as and assertions"
     },
     "p": {
      "ar": "**type assertion** `x as Order` = «صدقني يا tsc» — من غير أي فحص وقت التشغيل. استخدمها نادرًا (DOM: `as HTMLInputElement`)، ومتستخدمهاش على بيانات من برة. والـ `!` (non-null) نفس الكلام: بيسكّت tsc، مش بيحميك.",
      "en": "A **type assertion** `x as Order` = «trust me, tsc» — with no runtime check at all. Use it rarely (the DOM: `as HTMLInputElement`), and never on outside data. The `!` (non-null) is the same: it silences tsc, it does not protect you."
     },
     "ex": "const input = document.querySelector(\"#qty\") as HTMLInputElement;   // fine: you wrote the HTML\nconst qty = Number(input.value);\n\nconst order = (await res.json()) as Order;     // ✗ no check: a wrong reply crashes later\nconst checked = OrderSchema.parse(await res.json());   // ✓ validated (Zod, next week)",
     "lang": "ts"
    },
    {
     "h": {
      "ar": "as const وsatisfies",
      "en": "as const and satisfies"
     },
     "p": {
      "ar": "**as const** بيخلّي الكائن أو المصفوفة readonly بقيم literal — بديل الـ enum: `const STATUSES = [\"new\", \"paid\"] as const` ومنها `type Status = typeof STATUSES[number]`. و**satisfies** بيتأكد إن قيمة مطابقة لنوع **من غير** ما يضيّع أنواعها الدقيقة.",
      "en": "**as const** makes an object or array readonly with literal values — the enum replacement: `const STATUSES = [\"new\", \"paid\"] as const` and from it `type Status = typeof STATUSES[number]`. **satisfies** checks a value matches a type **without** losing its precise types."
     },
     "ex": "const STATUSES = [\"new\", \"paid\", \"shipped\"] as const;\ntype Status = typeof STATUSES[number];            // \"new\" | \"paid\" | \"shipped\"\n\nconst isStatus = (s: string): s is Status => (STATUSES as readonly string[]).includes(s);\n\nconst COLORS = { new: \"#888\", paid: \"#0a7d32\", shipped: \"#1a5fb4\" } satisfies Record<Status, string>;\n// forgetting \"shipped\" above → tsc error; and COLORS.paid stays the literal \"#0a7d32\"\n\nfor (const s of [\"paid\", \"lost\"]) console.log(s, isStatus(s) ? COLORS[s] : \"unknown status\");",
     "node": 1,
     "ts": 1
    }
   ],
